### 21.10 Safe Unload on Cancellation

**Requirement**: Generate code that properly cleans up resources when models are unloaded or cancelled.

**Why Needed**:
- Prevents resource leaks (GPU memory, system memory)
- Ensures backend is properly deinitialized
- Critical for long-running workflows and multi-instance scenarios

**Transpiler Responsibilities**:
1. Generate Drop implementation for model structs
2. Implement graceful shutdown on cancellation signals
3. Ensure model is freed before backend deinitialization
4. Handle nested models/unloads properly

**Generated Code Pattern**:
```rust
impl Drop for LlamaCppModelProvider {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // Step 1: Free context first (depends on model)
        drop(std::mem::take(&mut self.ctx));

        // Step 2: Free model (depends on backend)
        drop(std::mem::take(&mut self.model));

        // Step 3: Backend cleanup (no longer any models)
        drop(std::mem::take(&mut self.backend));
    }
}

// Graceful shutdown on cancellation
async fn generate_with_cleanup(&self, prompt: &str) -> Result<String> {
    let mut stream = self.generate_stream(prompt).await?;
    let mut buffer = String::new();

    loop {
        tokio::select! {
            _ = tokio::signal::ctrl_c() => {
                // Safe cleanup on cancellation
                return Ok(buffer); // Return partial result
            }
            token = stream.next() => {
                match token {
                    Some(Ok(text)) => buffer.push_str(&text),
                    Some(Err(e)) => return Err(e),
                    None => break, // End of stream
                }
            }
        }
    }

    Ok(buffer)
}
```

